name: variables
boss: $VARIABLE
flavor: I hold what you've forgotten.
brief: A variable is name=value, with no spaces around the =. You read it back
brief: with $name. Command substitution $(cmd) runs a command and becomes its
brief: output, which is how you get a number out of wc and into a comparison.
brief:
brief: Quote your expansions. "$x" is one word even when the value has spaces;
brief: $x is however many words the value happens to split into. Nearly every
brief: shell script bug that survives review is a missing pair of quotes.
---
q: {store the word @word@ in a variable called @var@|put @word@ into a variable named @var@|make a variable called @var@ hold the word @word@}
a: @var@=@word@
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: no spaces around the equals sign
---
q: %show% the value of @var@
a: @var@=@word@; echo $@var@
out: @word@
ref: echo
teach: echo
hint: $name (or ${name}) is how you read a variable back
---
q: {store the output of the date command in a variable called @tvar@|capture what date prints into a variable named @tvar@|put the result of running date into @tvar@}
a: @tvar@=$(date)
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: $( ) runs a command and becomes its output
---
q: {store the number of lines in @log@ in a variable called @cvar@|capture how many lines @log@ has into a variable named @cvar@|put @log@'s line count into a variable called @cvar@}
a: @cvar@=$(wc -l < @log@)
ref: wc
teach: wc -l
hint: command substitution feeds wc's count straight into the assignment
---
q: %show% the length of the value in @var@
a: @var@=@word@; echo ${#@var@}
out: [0-9]
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: a hash inside the braces gives the length
---
q: %show% @var@, or the word default if it is unset or empty
a: echo ${@var@:-default}
out: default
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: :- substitutes default only when the variable is unset or empty
---
q: %show% the line count of @log@ inside a sentence reading "lines: N"
a: echo "lines: $(wc -l < @log@)"
out: ^lines: [0-9]+$
ref: echo
teach: echo
hint: $( ) expands fine inside double quotes, right beside literal text
---
q: {export @mvar@ with the value @word@ so child processes inherit it|set @mvar@ to @word@ and export it so children see it|make @mvar@=@word@ part of the environment, not just this shell}
a: export @mvar@=@word@
ref: export
teach: export
hint: export is what makes a variable visible to child processes
---
q: %show% every exported variable in the environment
a: env
out: =
ref: env
teach: env
hint: env with no arguments dumps the whole environment
---
q: {run one command with @mvar@ set to @word@, without changing your own shell|run a single command that sees @mvar@=@word@ while your shell stays untouched|give just one command @mvar@=@word@ without exporting it yourself}
a: env @mvar@=@word@ sh -c 'echo $@mvar@'
out: @word@
ref: env
teach: env
hint: VAR=value cmd sets it for that command alone, not your shell
---
q: {run a command with the environment completely emptied first|run a command that starts with no inherited environment at all|strip the environment down to nothing before running a command}
a: env -i sh -c 'echo empty'
out: empty
ref: env
teach: env
hint: -i drops the entire inherited environment before running the command
---
q: {run a command with @mvar@ removed from the environment|run a command that cannot see @mvar@|hide @mvar@ from a single command without touching your own shell}
a: env -u @mvar@ sh -c 'echo gone'
out: gone
ref: env
teach: env -u
hint: -u strips one variable out of the environment for that command only
---
q: %show% the environment with entries separated by NUL rather than newline
a: env -0
out: =
ref: env
teach: env -0
hint: -0 separates entries with NUL instead of a newline
---
q: {remove the variable @var@ from your shell entirely|delete @var@ so it is no longer set at all|make @var@ fully unset, not just empty}
a: unset @var@
ref: unset
teach: unset
hint: unset removes the variable entirely, unlike setting it to an empty string
---
q: {read a line from standard input into a variable called @var@|take one line of input and store it in @var@|capture the next line typed into a variable named @var@}
a: read @var@
ref: read
teach: read
hint: read takes one line from stdin and stores it in the named variable
---
q: {read a line into @var@ without letting backslashes escape anything|read one line into @var@ while leaving backslashes literal|take a line of input into @var@, raw, with no backslash escaping}
a: read -r @var@
ref: read
teach: read -r
hint: -r is what you want essentially always; without it a backslash vanishes
---
q: {prompt with "name: " and read the reply into @var@|show "name: " then read what comes back into @var@|ask "name: " and store the answer in @var@}
a: read -p "name: " @var@
ref: read
teach: read -p
hint: -p prints its argument as a prompt before reading
---
q: %show% the first field of @csv@, using a variable to hold the separator
a: d=,; cut -d"$d" -f1 @csv@
out: ^gateway$
ref: cut
teach: cut -d
hint: the variable holding the separator still needs quoting when it's used
---
q: {run a single command in a child shell using the -c flag|use sh -c to run one command in a fresh child shell|spawn a child shell just to execute one command with -c}
a: sh -c 'echo @word@'
out: ^@word@$
ref: sh
teach: sh -c
hint: -c takes the command to run as its next argument
---
q: {remove every alias at once|clear out all aliases in one command|wipe every alias you have defined}
a: unalias -a
ref: unalias
teach: unalias -a
hint: -a clears every alias in one shot
---
q: {describe what ls is, in a full sentence rather than just a path|explain in words what ls resolves to|ask command for a plain-english description of ls}
a: command -V ls
out: ls
ref: command
teach: command -V
hint: -V gives a full descriptive sentence rather than just a path
---
q: %find% ls using a safe default PATH rather than whatever yours happens to be
a: command -p ls @txt@
out: @txt@
ref: command
teach: command -p
hint: -p ignores your PATH and uses the system's standard one instead
---
q: %show% every place in your PATH that has a command called sh
a: which -a sh
out: sh
ref: which
teach: which -a
hint: -a lists every match in PATH, not just the first one found
